Understanding Virtualization
  
  Virtualization uses software (a hypervisor) to simulate hardware, obscuring physical hardware from applications/operating systems and
  creating virtual versions of compute, storage, and networking resources. Client-side virtualization runs on a user's own workstation — the
  hypervisor runs as an application on top of the host OS, which stays in control of the hardware. A virtual machine (its OS, config, data,
  apps) exists as a file (or set of files) on the host, making it easy to copy, back up, and clone to another host.
  
  Benefits: multiple OS environments on one physical device (cost savings), increased security (apps isolated by the hypervisor), better
  resource utilization, easier disaster recovery (copy the file, restore it elsewhere), and simplified workload migration between physical
  hardware.
  
  Challenges: hypervisor is an extra software layer consuming RAM/storage/CPU; compromising the hypervisor can compromise every environment
  it hosts; extra licensing costs (apps, OS, and hypervisor); and hardware compatibility requirements.

The Purpose of Virtual Machines (VMs)
  
  A VM is the software-based execution of a physical computer — the guest OS behaves as if running on real hardware. The hypervisor intercepts
  OS/hardware calls and allocates physical resources (CPU, memory, storage, networking) to each VM, isolating VMs from each other (a security
  barrier) and allowing dynamic resource allocation.
  
  Key facts: VMs are stored as virtual disk files, enabling snapshots (point-in-time rollback), cloning, and easy migration between hosts.
  A type 1 hypervisor IS the host OS (bare metal — used on servers/blade servers in data centers, managed remotely). A type 2 hypervisor
  runs inside an existing host OS (used for testing/isolated environments on client machines).
  
  Common use cases: cloud computing (VMs are the backbone), software testing, running legacy/vulnerable systems in isolation, cybersecurity
  sandboxing, and educational lab environments.

Desktop Virtualization
  
  Runs multiple desktop environments on one physical machine or via cloud-hosted services. Two types:
    
    - Local desktop virtualization: multiple desktops on a single local session (e.g., switching between "accounting" and "sales" desktops).
    - Remote desktop virtualization (VDI — Virtual Desktop Infrastructure): desktops hosted centrally (on-prem or cloud) and accessed remotely
      via client software or a browser; a hypervisor on the server splits physical hardware into VMs, each hosting multiple desktop sessions.
    
  VDI sessions can be persistent (user settings saved between sessions) or non-persistent (desktop resets each time — good for
  kiosks/education). Benefits: cost efficiency (client hardware doesn't need to meet app requirements), improved security
  (apps/data never touch the local device), centralized management, and flexible access from any device/location.
  
  Use cases: remote workers using corporate apps, call centers/data-entry roles needing a locked-down interface, consistent educational lab
  environments, high-security industries, and software development/testing needing a stable, repeatable environment.
  
  Challenges: high initial setup cost (or higher ongoing cost with a cloud solution), dependency on network reliability and latency, scaling
  limitations/costs, and potential compliance issues if data resides in the cloud outside required jurisdictions.

Containers
  
  Containers are lightweight, standalone executable packages that include the application code plus everything needed to run it (runtime,
  tools, libraries, dependencies) — but they share the host OS kernel rather than including a full OS. This makes them much lighter and
  faster than VMs.
  
  Key components: the container runtime (defines what's needed to run the app, pulling images/settings from a registry), and Kubernetes,
  an open-source orchestration tool for automatically deploying, scaling, and managing containers at scale.
  
  Containers vs. VMs: containers start in seconds (VMs take minutes), use fewer resources, and are ideal for cloud-native apps,
  microservices, and DevOps/CI-CD workflows. VMs are still the right choice for running different OS environments, legacy apps, or
  providing IaaS.
  
  Challenges: less isolation than a VM (shared kernel); usually non-persistent (data isn't saved when the container stops, unless persistent
  storage is configured); lifecycle management can get complex without orchestration tools; and containers depend on kernel compatibility —
  Docker solves this by presenting a homogeneous kernel environment across different host kernels.

Type I vs. Type II Hypervisors
  
  - Type I (bare metal): installed directly on hardware, acting as the OS itself. Minimal local interface (often text-based);
  managed remotely via a control station. More performant, smaller attack surface (no underlying OS to exploit), supports advanced
  features like live migration. Used in data centers/production/enterprise environments — the backbone of cloud computing.
  
  - Type II (hosted): installed as an application on top of an existing host OS (Windows, Linux, macOS); requests pass through the
  host OS. Easier to use and more flexible/compatible, but has higher performance overhead and inherits the host OS's security
  vulnerabilities. Used for testing, training, and development on client workstations.
  
  Considerations when choosing: deployment complexity and training needs, hardware sizing (don't just add up guest OS requirements — plan
  extra capacity for the hypervisor itself), the hypervisor as a potential single point of failure/attack, and additional licensing costs.

Virtualization Security Requirements
  
  Key risks specific to virtualization:
  
    - Hypervisor vulnerabilities: errors in hypervisor code can allow an attacker to escape the VM boundary — known as a VM escape attack.
    - Misconfigured VMs: default passwords, excessive user privileges, or misconfigured rights are easy attack vectors.
    - Virtual disk theft: since a VM is just a file, an attacker who gains access can steal a complete copy of a production system.
    - Denial-of-service (DoS): a hypervisor flaw could let an attacker over-provision resources until the physical hardware can't keep up,
      denying service to everyone.
  
  Best practices: regular patching of hypervisor, host OS, and guest OSes (ideally automated); least privilege access control; encryption
  of data at rest and secure transmission protocols; network segmentation; monitoring/logging (increasingly automated, since VMs are
  created/deleted dynamically); and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) for hypervisor management tasks (VM creation, migration, etc.), with
  all such changes audited.
  
  Future trends: AI-assisted threat detection, zero trust principles applied to virtualization infrastructure, container-specific security
  strategies distinct from traditional tools, cloud-native vendor security solutions, and automated security enforcement given the sheer
  scale of virtual assets.

Virtualization Network and Storage Requirements
  
  Networking:
    
    - Software-Defined Networking (SDN): separates the control plane (configuration/decision logic, now centralized in software) from the
      data plane (the actual physical devices moving data). SDN layers: application layer (firewall rules, routing, load balancing), control
      layer (central software instructing devices), and infrastructure layer (physical switches/routers).
    - Network Function Virtualization (NFV): simulates physical network devices in software, within the hypervisor, to create virtual
      networks between VMs.
    - VLANs: segregated network address groups — isolated and secure by default, requiring explicit rules to communicate across VLANs.
    - VPNs: encrypted point-to-point connections creating a private network over shared physical infrastructure.
    
  Storage:
    
    - Software-Defined Storage (SDS): virtualizes physical storage so VMs see a consistent "disk" regardless of the underlying physical
      hardware; the hypervisor translates requests to the actual physical disk.
    - SAN (Storage Area Network): high-performance, low-latency network storage, typically for databases.
    - NAS (Network-Attached Storage): network storage typically used for file sharing.
    - Storage virtualization methods: block storage virtualization (abstracts storage blocks, enables snapshots/replication transparently
      to the guest OS), file storage virtualization (presents files as a local file list, supports centralized file management), and
      object storage virtualization (flat, non-hierarchical storage — ideal for large-scale cloud/media/backup storage).
    
  Benefits: near-limitless scalability, better hardware utilization (shared but isolated workloads), faster disaster recovery (just
  copy/restart the VM file), easier network segmentation, and automation/orchestration through software.
  
  Challenges: added performance overhead from the extra software layer, the hypervisor as a central point of attack, hardware that may not
  support virtualization, new/unfamiliar management tooling, and regulatory compliance complexity around data's physical location
  (especially in the cloud).

Installing and Configuring a Hypervisor (Demo — Hyper-V on Windows)

Hyper-V (a type II hypervisor when installed on top of Windows) is supported on all Windows editions except Windows Home.
Three ways to install it:
  
  1. PowerShell (as administrator): Enable-WindowsOptionalFeature -Online -FeatureName Microsoft-Hyper-V -All
  2. DISM (as administrator, via Command Prompt): DISM /Online /Enable-Feature /All /FeatureName:Microsoft-Hyper-V
  3. GUI: Control Panel → "Turn Windows features on or off" → check both Hyper-V Management Tools and Hyper-V Platform.

All three methods require a reboot to complete installation. After reboot, Hyper-V Manager connects automatically to the local machine.

Key configuration step: in Hyper-V Settings, set where virtual hard disks and VM configuration files are stored — ideally on a separate
volume/disk from the host OS to avoid disk contention.

Creating a Virtual Machine (VM) — Demo (Hyper-V)

Using the New Virtual Machine Wizard in Hyper-V Manager:
  
  1. Specify Name and storage Location (ideally a non-OS volume).
  2. Choose Generation: Generation 2 for modern 64-bit hardware with UEFI BIOS; Generation 1 for older/legacy compatibility.
  3. Assign Memory — balance the VM's needs against what's left for the host, based on the guest OS's minimum requirements.
  4. Configure Networking: "Not Connected" (isolated, no networking) or "Default Switch" (NATs through the host's network adapter to
     provide internet/network access).
  5. Connect Virtual Hard Disk: create a new virtual disk, use an existing one, or attach one later — factor in storage needs for the
     OS and any applications.
  6. Installation Options: install an OS immediately (e.g., from an ISO image mounted as a virtual DVD drive) or install later.

Once created, the VM can be configured further (hardware, security, memory, processor, etc.) and started/connected to install the guest OS.

Course Summary

This course covered client-side virtualization: the advantages of VMs (sandboxing, testing, app deployment), desktop virtualization/VDI,
containers vs. VMs, type I vs. type II hypervisors, virtualization security, and installing/managing hypervisors and VMs (demonstrated
with Hyper-V).

Quick Exam-Focus Reminders

- Type 1 hypervisor = bare metal, IS the OS, used in production/data centers. Type 2 hypervisor = hosted, runs on top of an existing OS,
  used for testing/training on client machines.
- A VM is a complete OS + virtual hardware via the hypervisor; a container shares the host OS kernel and only packages the app + its
  dependencies — containers start in seconds, VMs take minutes.
- Kubernetes = container orchestration; Docker = presents a consistent kernel environment across hosts for containers.
- VDI persistent = settings saved between sessions; non-persistent = resets each session (kiosks, labs).
- VM escape attack = an attacker breaks out of a VM's isolation via a hypervisor flaw — the top hypervisor-specific security risk.
- RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) and least privilege are the core access-control principles for hypervisor management.
- SDN separates the control plane (configuration logic) from the data plane (actual traffic forwarding); NFV virtualizes network devices
  in software.
- SAN = high-performance block storage over network (databases); NAS = file-sharing network storage.
- Block, file, and object storage virtualization are the three storage virtualization methods — object storage is flat/non-hierarchical,
  ideal for large-scale cloud/media data.
- Hyper-V install methods: PowerShell (Enable-WindowsOptionalFeature), DISM, or the GUI ("Turn Windows features on or off") — all require a
  reboot.
